= 2. Lexical structure

== 2.1 Program text

This chapter describes the *core* token surface. A program is a sequence of
whitespace-separated tokens. There is no semicolon, no significant newline, and
no comment form: the language is word-oriented, every word stands on its own
with its own semantics, and a source reads like regular language (D131).
Whitespace is a separator, never part of a word.

The prose surface has its own lexical rules (17.7): punctuation, lowercase
keywords, text with spaces, and names canonicalized through the vocabulary.
== 2.2 Token classes

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, auto),
  align: (left, left, left),
  table.header([Form], [Meaning], [Status]),
  [`42`, `-1`, `3.5`], [a real number, parsed as a 64-bit float], [Implemented],
  [`"text"`], [a single-word text literal; the body is the characters between the quotes], [Implemented],
  [`:name`], [an atom literal; the reference to a named atom], [Implemented],
  [`?name`], [a unification variable or pattern variable], [Implemented],
  [`^?name`], [a pinned pattern variable], [Implemented],
  [`!?name`], [a shadowing pattern variable], [Implemented],
  [`_`], [a wildcard pattern], [Implemented],
  [`{`, `}`], [a quotation, collected at run time], [Implemented],
  [`[`, `]`], [an extension set literal], [Implemented],
  [any other token], [a word call], [Implemented],
)

In the core surface a text literal is one whitespace-free word: whitespace is
the word separator, so a quoted string containing spaces is not a single token
(D131). The prose surface admits text with spaces and keeps its case (17.7).

== 2.3 Reserved and structural tokens

The following are syntax, not names, and are never shadowed by a lexical
binding: `{`, `}`, `[`, `]`, numbers, text literals, `:name`, `?name`, and the
internal return tokens (a leading NUL). Every other token is a name and may be
shadowed by a lexical binding (D122).

== 2.4 Source positions

Every executed token carries a location, baseline, and position. A diagnostic
reports them with the error. The character offset for a source violation is
recorded when a source form is available (D76).
= 3. Surface syntax and evaluation shape

== 3.1 Concatenative core

Evaluation is stack-based. A token that is not syntax is a word call. Words pop
their operands from the stack and push their results. There is no implicit
argument order beyond the stack: the last pushed value is the top.

The core words are the arithmetic and comparison words (`add`, `subtract`,
`multiply`, `divide`, `lessThan`, `greaterThan`, `equals`), the stack words
(`duplicate`, `swap`, `drop`, `over`, `rotate`), and the control words below.
The canonical spelling is camelCase for the comparison words; the lowered IR
also accepts `lessThan` and `greaterThan` as the token names (D124).

These are the core-surface spellings. The prose surface writes lowercase
words with spaces and canonicalizes them through the vocabulary (`less than`
becomes `lessThan`) (17.7).
== 3.2 Quotations

`{` collects tokens up to the matching `}` into a text quotation and pushes its
address. A quotation is data: it is not executed until `apply` or a control word
runs it. Nested quotations are collected by depth.

```text
5 { duplicate } apply add     // 10
```

== 3.3 `if`

The surface is `condition { then } { else } if`. `if` pops the else quotation,
the then quotation, and the condition scalar, then splices the chosen quotation
into the program. A zero condition chooses the else branch; a non-zero condition
chooses the then branch. The condition MUST be a scalar.

== 3.4 `apply`

`apply` pops a quotation address or a closure and runs it. A bare quotation runs
in the caller's current frame (dynamic scope). A closure runs in its captured
frame and restores the caller's frame through the internal return token. An
`apply` with no target is a stack underflow, not a silent no-op.

== 3.5 `define` and `control`

`define` is the global word definition. The stack form `body "name" define`
stores the body quotation under the name in the user-word table. The definition
words read the name and quotation body from the program:

```text
logic name { body }
cycle name { body }
cycle first { body } and second { body }
```

`logic` defines a non-recursive word. Its own name is not in scope in its body,
so a self-reference is a named refusal. `cycle` defines a recursive word; the
body uses the plain word name, with no separate `recurse` word. `and` groups
`cycle` definitions: every group name is in scope in every group body, and the
group is stored together. A later definition under the same name wins. `define`
remains available as the stack form.

`control` is the form declaration: `handler "name" control` captures the current
frame and installs a code cell as the implementation of the form. A later
declaration under the same name wins. The default control forms (`{`, `if`,
`apply`, `define`, `logic`, `cycle`) are seeded as image-resident words whose
implementations name the matching kernel primitive.
== 3.6 `let` and `lambda`

`value "name" let` pops the name and the value, creates a child frame containing
the binding, and makes that frame current. It produces no value. The binding is
visible to a bare token (D122) and to `lookup`; `lookup` walks the frame chain
and then the global binding table.

`{ body } lambda` captures the current frame in an image-resident closure and
pushes it. A closure applied with `apply` runs its body in the captured frame and
then restores the caller's frame.

== 3.7 Word resolution order

For a bare token that is not syntax or a literal, resolution is:

+ a control form (D21, D51);
+ the nearest lexical frame binding (D122);
+ a built-in named word;
+ a host word;
+ a word metaobject, generic function (D19), user word (`define`), or
  vocabulary entry;
+ otherwise `Unresolved(Word, token)`.

A lexical binding therefore shadows a built-in word, but syntax and literals are
never shadowed. An inactive word is `InactiveWord`, never a silent fallthrough.

Each resolved call produces one `Callable` kind and invokes through one
`Invoke`. Control primitives and closures, builtin tokens, host words, word
values, generics, user words, and vocabulary entries therefore share one call
boundary. Builtin tokens still dispatch through the primitive table.
== 3.8 Failures

A failure is a value, not an exception, in the interpreter: every word returns
`Result<EvaluationState, EvaluationError>`, and the main loop attaches source
position and returns a `Diagnostic`. A named reason is matchable; an untyped
string refusal is not part of the language. The compiled path converts a runtime
failure at the harness boundary into a named fault and the caller maps it back to
a diagnostic.

== 3.9 History, bookmarks, and rollback

Event-stream recording is opt-in and off by default. `history-on` enables it and
`history-off` disables it; a bookmark records its own step explicitly. While
recording is on, every executed token appends a step recording the token, the
stack before it, the program to resume, the location/baseline/position, and the
previous step. The steps form a persistent chain, so appending is O(1) and
`history`/`rollback` walk the chain. `history` reports the steps, `rollback`
restores one, and a bookmark is a named step. These are interpreter features.
A compiled artifact that carries a step record replays its history (D201,
superseding D120): the lowered instruction set has a `RecordStep` instruction, a
resumable position (`LoweredAbstraction.runFrom`), and `LoweredInstructions.scopeOf`
reports `HistoryRecorded` for a program that contains one. A program without a
step record is `HistoryExcluded`, which stays the default the emitter writes.
Recording is an emitter choice, not a side effect of lowering: the default
`EvaluatorLowering.lowerExpression` emits no record, and
`EvaluatorLowering.lowerExpressionWithStepRecords` emits one at the start of each
statement of a multi-statement `Block` or `Quotation` body. A kernel step is a
resume point, not a rollback point: it records a position only, so a resume is
sound only when the caller supplies the state that position had.

A code value is `[QuoteTag; documentId]`, and the text at `documentId` is the
document that says what the value is. Two forms are accepted (D202): a
`term-program` document, and an `instruction-program` document, which uses the
image opcode spelling so a `RecordStep` survives it. Each form has its own
reader, and a reader refuses the other form by name instead of decoding it as its
own. The instruction form is the one D202 keeps.

Moving the interpreter onto that encoding needs one opcode the instruction set
does not have. The interpreter runs three shapes: a number, a word, and a
`Quote`, which turns a token body into a code value at run time. The first two
already map (`LiteralNumber`, `CallWord`); the third becomes
`MakeCode of body: Instruction list`, written as
`{"op": "make-code", "instructions": [...]}` in the image and in the instruction
document. It makes a code value from its body exactly as the interpreter's
`Quote` does, so a quotation is code in both encodings, and it closes the one
`UnitOnly` ledger entry that a lowering target can close. The op is additive: an
older reader refuses it by name, which is the documented behaviour for every new
opcode.

== 3.10 The F\#-equivalent pipeline

The pipeline operator, #raw("|>"), is the F\#-equivalent pipeline. It exists for
a 1:1 port of F\# code, not for the concatenative core. It is left-associative
and binds looser than a call: #raw("x |> f(a) |> g(b)") means
#raw("(x |> f(a)) |> g(b)").

F\# passes the piped value as the *last* argument. The elaborations are:

+ #raw("x |> f(a)") becomes #raw("f(a, x)").
+ #raw("x |> f") becomes #raw("f(x)").
+ #raw("x |> f(a) |> g(b)") becomes #raw("g(b, f(a, x))").

The two argument positions are independent of any language: `LastArgument` is
the default, and `FirstArgument` is available for surfaces that use it. F\# is
the compatibility case for `LastArgument`; Elixir is the compatibility case for
`FirstArgument`. A `Pipeline` node records the style so a front-end or adapter
can choose either without changing the surface or the kernel.

The surface parses a value, the operator, and a stage. A stage is a word, a
call, or a word followed by whitespace-separated stage arguments:
#raw("x |> f(a)") and #raw("x |> f a") both elaborate to
#raw("Call(f, [a; x])"). A pipeline without a stage is `ExpectedExpression`. A
stage that is not callable is the normal call-target refusal at lowering, never
a silent token splice.

Stage arguments are a pipeline-surface convenience, not general currying.
Outside a pipeline, a bare application #raw("f a") remains unavailable. The
pipeline supplies the final argument; first-class functions and general partial
application are separate work.

The `Pipeline` node is preserved in the portable expression document. Lowering
desugars it to `Call`; the node keeps the source offset and the style for
diagnostics and a later Elixir-style elaboration.

== 3.11 Explicit partial application

`_` in a call argument is a hole. A call with at least one hole is a `Partial`
expression, not a complete call:

```text
subtract(_ 10)        a function of one argument
map(_ xs)             a function of one argument
f(_ a _)              a function of two arguments, in hole order
```

The supplied arguments are evaluated once, when the partial value is created.
The holes bind when the partial value is applied. The result is an ordinary
closure: `apply` runs it, and `x |> f(a)` remains a separate pipeline rule.

This is explicit partial application, not automatic currying. `_` outside a
call argument is a named word, not a hole. A bare #raw("f a") outside a pipeline
is still refused, and there is no implicit "supply the missing arguments"
rule. A call with no holes is a complete `Call`.

The `Partial` node is preserved in the portable expression document. Lowering
turns it into a closure whose parameters are the holes and whose body is a call
to the target; the supplied arguments are bound in the enclosing frame so they
are evaluated once. The target must be a word or a lambda in the current
lowered subset; any other target is the normal call-target refusal.

== 3.12 The reverse pipe

The reverse pipe, #raw("<|"), exists only to run F\# packages that use it. It is
*discouraged*: it reads against the dataflow, and the forward pipeline is the
preferred surface.

Semantics are the reverse of #raw("|>"):

+ #raw("f <| x") means #raw("f(x)").
+ #raw("f a <| x") means #raw("f(a, x)").
+ #raw("f <| g <| x") is right-associative and means #raw("f(g(x))").

The expression is a `ReversePipeline` node, preserved in the portable document.
Lowering appends the value to the left call, or wraps a plain target as a
one-argument call. Supplied partial arguments already on the left keep their
positions.

The parser gives #raw("<|") the lowest precedence and right associativity; it is
not a synonym for #raw("|>") and does not use `LastArgument`/`FirstArgument`.
Automatic currying remains out of scope.

== 3.13 Type elaboration (P4, core)

Type inference is optional elaboration, not runtime evaluation. P4 derives a type
and an effect set from the expression using the internal word registry and
the package-boundary FCS read-off, or returns a named diagnostic. There is no
user-facing signature syntax; inference never observes a running value to choose
a type.

P4a provides the host-side type core: type variables, primitives, sorts,
functions with latent effects, open record rows with lacks constraints, schemes,
substitutions, constraints, an occurs-check, generalization, instantiation, and
named errors. An effect set is closed or open (`Open(known, SetVariable)`); a
scheme and a word signature quantify type variables, effect variables, and record
row variables; function unification uses the set unifier; record unification
uses the row unifier with lacks constraints; and a call adds a block's latent
effects through set unification. Closed effect subtraction is implemented; open
effect subtraction, typed handlers, and effect rows are not part of P4a. P4b
checks these expression forms against the registry:

+ literals, words, calls, lambdas, application, and partial application with
  argument holes;
+ `Let` binding nodes with sound generalization (the `logic` surface spelling is
  still a design);
+ `if` branches with a unified result type;
+ blocks and pipelines.

The internal primitive registry lives in
`components/prelude/evaluator/signatures.fs`. It types `add`, `subtract`,
`multiply`, `divide`, `lessThan`, `greaterThan`, and `equals` as implementation
metadata, not source annotations. F\# package bindings get their types from FCS
read-off.

P4c adds a parallel expression source map, `InferenceDiagnostic` with a path and
an optional `SourceRange`, and the opt-in `lowerExpressionWithInference`
handoff. `parseLocated` supplies the root range and precise call/partial target
and argument paths; other child paths are the next range slice.
`scale --prose-typecheck PATH` parses, infers, and lowers without running. A
type diagnostic stops lowering; the existing lowering refusal remains a separate
layer. The canonical expression tree and portable document remain range-free.

P4 does not provide effect rows, type-level lambdas, dependent types, or
pattern-match inference. Closed subtyping is used for application arguments and
`if` branch joins; it is not a general subtype checker. An unknown primitive or
expression is refused; ambiguous multiple dispatch is resolved from inferred
arguments and is a diagnostic when undecidable. Effects are exact sets, not rows.
Record rows are open and lack-aware; they are not effect rows. Child source
ranges and the
`logic`/anonymous-function surface remain open; the core and root located path are
implemented, while the complete surface remains partial.

== 3.14 F\# abstraction and manifest

`FSharpAbstraction` is a versioned in-memory subset for the Prelude and F\#
packages: a module name, version, source hash, and bindings. A binding carries a
name, parameters, an expression body, the plain names it calls, a source range,
and the F\# recursion kind (`non-recursive`, `recursive`, or
`mutually-recursive`). The abstraction is not a second runtime; it lowers to the
same `PackageUnit`/`PackageUnitGroup` records used by books and `logic`/`cycle`
words.

A manifest has schema version `1`, a package name, a mode (`automatic`, `mixed`,
or `strict`), and per-binding declarations. A declaration kind is
`non-recursive`, `recursive`, or `mutually-recursive` with a `mutual` name list.
A `reason` is `uninitialized-value`, `cross-module`, `value-restriction`, or
`other:`. In `strict` mode every binding must be declared; `mixed` accepts
declarations for some bindings; `automatic` needs none.

An explicit declaration must agree with the F\# kind. A disagreement without a
reason is `ManifestDisagreement`; a disagreement with a reason is accepted and
tracked as an override. The resulting classification record carries the binding
name and `BindingKind`; it has no tier field. `FS0040`, the unused-recursive
warning, and the value-restriction warning map to Scale diagnostic codes before
they reach a user. Metadata crossing IPC is `(name, kind, version)` only; a
function body is never serialized.

The product and AOT runtime do not link FCS. FCS is a build-time dependency. Its
adapter emits a `ReadOffModule` with names, parameters, call names, source
ranges, recursion kinds, and bodies for the supported expression subset:
literals, names, calls, lambdas, `let`, `if`, sequential blocks, and tuple/list
blocks. `fromReadOff` combines that record with a hand-supplied body when one is
present. An unsupported F\# expression or pattern is a named refusal, never a
guessed translation. A schema mismatch is a named refusal.
= 4. Values and the image

== 4.1 Identity

An `EntryIdentity` is a distinct reference into the store lexicon. It is a
struct wrapping an integer and is not a host integer in the language: it projects
to a rank-0 tensor at a boundary. Identity equality is reference equality.

== 4.2 Tensors

The only element type of the value domain is a real number. A `Tensor` is a
`Data` array plus a `Shape`. A scalar is a rank-0 tensor. Tensors are immutable:
an operation produces a new tensor and a new store entry. Tensor order is
significant; `Tensor.append` appends at the end, so the stack top is the last
element.

== 4.3 Text and atoms

Text is stored as a sequence of character codes in a tensor and interned in the
store's text cache. `TextRead` returns the interned text; `TextEquals` compares
two text entries. Atoms are text entries reached through `:name`; they are
distinct from text literals only by their surface form.

== 4.4 The store

The store owns the lexicon, the text cache, the next index, the association
tables, and the named roots. It is append-only. An update writes a new vector and
re-points a root; it never rewrites or truncates an entry. The association-vector
primitives (`addToEnvironment`, `findPair`, root add/set) are the environment
substrate.

The execution-root registry (`RootKind`/`rootKinds`) names `VocabularyTable`,
`UserWordTable`, `BindingTable`, `SetTable`, `DeclarationTable`,
`GenericFunctionTable`, `WordTable`, `HistoryTable`, `ClassTable`,
`InstanceStateTable`, and `ChannelsRoot`. The current and saved frames are
control state, not registry roots. Roots are execution state except where a
feature explicitly makes them durable; a reader re-threads them. A new root
adds one `RootKind` case and the coverage test fails until it is in
`rootKinds` and `getRoot`/`setRoot`.

== 4.5 Durable data versus execution state

The image is the authority for durable and cross-actor values. Execution state
is never serialized: the stack, the current and saved frames, the scheduler and
actor state, ownership state, the event-stream history, handlers, and the
program counter. Persistence serializes only durable or promoted entries; it
does not serialize a stack or a frame.

== 4.6 Promotion and epochs

A value crossing an actor or persistence boundary is promoted to the image; it
must never leave a dangling reference into the sender's epoch. Promotion
reclassifies an entry as durable without moving it. Physical reclamation closes
a closed epoch's unpromoted entries when no live root reaches them. There is no
global mutable heap and no per-process tracing GC in this design.

= 5. Evaluation model

== 5.1 The loop

Evaluation is a token loop over a `Program` (a token list) and a stack. Each
token decrements the reduction budget, increments the consumed count, and
appends a history step. The loop is the single failure decision point: a word
failure is offered to the condition system; a supervised child becomes an exit;
an unsupervised actor aborts the run with a diagnostic.

== 5.2 Reductions and deadlines

`Reductions` is a fairness budget, not a failure. Exhausting it yields to the
next ready actor and resumes with a fresh budget. A `Deadline` is an actor's
token budget for its incarnation; exhaustion produces `TimeoutExit`, which the
supervisor decides. Neither is `ReductionExhausted`.

== 5.3 Control and call frames

`CurrentFrame` is the active lexical frame. `CallFrames` is the saved caller
chain. A closure call pushes the caller's frame and installs the captured frame;
the internal return token restores it. A return without a call is a named
refusal (`UnmatchedReturn`), never a silent no-op.

== 5.4 Actor steps

The scheduler owns the ready queue and the current actor. `spawn` creates a
child; `yield` gives the CPU to the next ready actor; `halt` ends normally;
`kill` ends with a reason. A supervised child's failure is an exit its supervisor
consumes; an unsupervised actor aborts the run. See chapter 10.

== 5.5 Failure attribution

A failure MUST be attributed to the layer that raised it: a kernel/abstraction failure, a
primitive failure, a Prelude word failure, and a parse failure are distinct. The
kernel flattens a typed error to `(code, message)` only at the C ABI edge. The
compiled harness raises a named fault and the caller maps it back to the same
diagnostic category.

= 6. Binding and scope

== 6.1 Frames

A frame is an image-resident association vector with a parent pointer. `let`
creates a child frame with one binding and makes it current. `lookup` walks the
chain (depth limit 1024) and then the global `BindingTable`. A frame is data; the
pointer to the current frame is execution state.

== 6.2 Bare names

A bare token resolves to the nearest lexical binding before it reaches the
built-in word table (D122). This is a language rule, not a lowering detail: the
interpreter and the lowered path MUST agree. A binding may therefore shadow a
built-in word. Syntax and literals are reserved and are never shadowed.

== 6.3 `lookup`

`name lookup` pops a name and pushes the binding. It searches the current frame
chain first, then the global binding table. An unbound name is
`Unresolved(Binding, name)`, never a silent false.

== 6.4 Closures

`lambda` captures the current frame. A closure's body runs in the captured frame;
the caller's frame is restored on return. A bare quotation applied with `apply`
keeps the caller's frame (dynamic scope). The distinction is observable and is
part of the specification.

== 6.5 Lowered slots

The lowered expression path represents a lexical binding twice: a frame binding
(for `lookup`) and a fresh slot (for a bare name). A lexically bound `Word`
lowers to `PushSlot`; `let` lowers value, name, `let`, `StoreSlot`. A lambda's
parameters are slots in a named unit; the unit prologue copies its frame into
slots. Capture is free-variable: only the enclosing names the body uses are
captured, accounting for parameters, inner `let` bindings, pattern variables, and
nested lambdas (D133).

== 6.6 Shadowing and duplicate names

A nested `let` shadows an outer binding; the nearest binding wins. Pattern
variables are scoped to their clause and shadow an outer binding. A duplicate
Fresh pattern variable in one clause is `DuplicatePatternVariable`; a duplicate
lambda parameter is `DuplicateParameter`. Bindings named by syntax or a literal
are refused with `ReservedBindingName`.

== 6.7 Calls to bound names

`Call(Word name, arguments)` lowers to a closure call when `name` is lexically
bound: the arguments are evaluated, the bound slot is pushed, and `CallClosure`
runs. The bound value must be a closure; a non-closure bound value is a named
lowered failure. This supersedes the earlier `CallTargetBound` refusal (D177) and
is what lets a `logic` function be called by name after its definition.

= 7. Pattern matching

== 7.1 Surface

```text
subject { pattern -> body | pattern -> body } match
```

`match` pops the clause quotation and the subject. Clauses are tried in order. A
clause that matches binds its variables and splices its body into the program.
If no clause matches, the result is `NoClauseMatched(subject)`, a named failure.

== 7.2 Pattern IR

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, auto),
  align: (left, left, left),
  table.header([Pattern], [Meaning], [Status]),
  [`Wildcard`], [`_`, matches anything, binds nothing], [Implemented],
  [`Variable(name, Fresh)`], [first occurrence; binds the subject], [Implemented],
  [`Variable(name, Repeated)`], [second occurrence; must unify with the first], [Implemented],
  [`Variable(name, Pin)`], [`^?name`; resolves through the binding table], [Implemented],
  [`Variable(name, Shadowing)`], [`!?name`; always rebinds], [Implemented],
  [`NumberLiteral`], [scalar equality], [Implemented],
  [`TextLiteral`], [text equality], [Implemented],
  [`AtomLiteral`], [atom text equality], [Implemented],
  [`Constructor(name, children)`], [constructor head and arity, then children], [Implemented],
  [`TypedPattern(sort, child)`], [typed term, then child], [Implemented],
)

== 7.3 Ground encoding

The matcher reads the same term encoding the unifier uses:

- a compound is a vector `[-2; head; argument; ...]`;
- a typed term is a vector `[-3; sort; term]`;
- an atom is any text entry that is not a `?name` variable.

A `Constructor` with no children also matches an atom with the same text; a
`Constructor` with children matches only a compound with the same head and arity.

== 7.4 Roles

`Fresh` binds on first occurrence. `Repeated` requires the name to be already
seen in the clause and unifies with the bound value. `Pin` reads the binding
table and unifies with it; a missing pin is `MissingPin`. `Shadowing` always
rebinds, including over an existing binding. A `Repeated` name that was not seen
is `UnboundRepeated`.

== 7.5 Guards

A guard is a named predicate with a pinned version: `subject { pattern when
predicate -> body } match`. The predicate runs in a fresh reduction budget with an
empty stack and an empty frame; a non-zero scalar is true, zero is false. A
predicate failure is `GuardFailed(name, reason)`, never a silent false. The
version is part of the portable contract; a guard whose pinned version is not
available is a refusal.

== 7.6 Open subjects

If the subject is an open unification variable and the pattern is not a wildcard
or variable, the matcher encodes the pattern as a term and unifies it with the
subject, then collects the substitutions. An undecidable case is
`PatternUndecided(reason)`, never a structural false. Shadowing on an open
subject is undecided.

== 7.7 Analysis

`PatternAnalysis` provides best-effort reachability, redundancy, and
closed-kind exhaustiveness. Best effort is explicitly not a proof: a pattern set
is not claimed exhaustive unless the analysis says so, and an analysis failure is
reported, not treated as a pass.

== 7.8 Lowered subset

The lowered expression path compiles ground patterns to inspection branches and
slots: wildcards, fresh variables, number/text/atom literals, constructors, and
typed patterns lower. `Pin`, `Repeated`, `Shadowing`, and guards are named
refusals; an open subject remains the interpreter's job. The clause body sees its
variables as lexical slots. See D125.

== 7.9 Host pattern boundary

The runtime pattern IR in 7.2 is the portable executable core. Host-specific
pattern features are represented outside it by `PatternHost.HostPattern`:

```text
Portable of Pattern
HostOnly of host: string * feature: string * children: HostPattern list
```

An `IHostPatternElaborator` lowers a `HostOnly` node into the portable core or
refuses it. Without an elaborator, the node is `HostSpecific(host, feature)`.
No `HostOnly` node reaches the runtime matcher or the portable pattern reader.
F\# active patterns, type tests, and host-specific guards, and Elixir bit syntax,
open maps, and host-specific guards, remain host-only until their elaborators
provide a faithful portable lowering.

= 8. Sets, sorts, and unification

== 8.1 Extension sets

`[ ... ]` is an extension literal: the body is evaluated and the resulting values
are admitted to a set. `[]` is the empty set. A value set is an image-resident
structure with a latest-write-wins association vector; the roots are
`SetsRoot`, `DeclarationsRoot`, and `GenericFunctionsRoot`.

== 8.2 Naming and sealing

`S :Name seal` seals a set under a name; `:Name` is a reference. A sealed set is
nominal: equality reads the name. An unsealed set is structural: equality reads
the extension. `unname` removes the name. A predicate seal and a constructor seal
are the named-predicate and named-constructor forms.

== 8.3 Set operations

`and`, `or`, and `nor` are the lattice operations. `set-union`, `set-intersect`,
`set-except`, `set-member`, `set-complement`, `same-shape`, `same-name`,
`sort-of`, `merge`, `filter`, and `set-subset` are the set words. `is` is
membership. `model` declares a domain; `admit` extends it. Membership deduplicates:
a set has no multiplicity and no order.

== 8.4 Sorts

A sort is a pair `(N : option Name, X ⊆ U)`. Nominal equality reads `N`;
structural equality reads `X` (extensionality). The operations are the dial
between the two readings:

- narrowing adds or relies on `N`, making two structurally identical sets
  distinct;
- widening drops `N`, making two names for the same set the same;
- restricting or enlarging `X` moves the structural projection.

The set operations are the teeth of the dial. `set-subset` is the value-level
subset operation; the type-level subtype relation is used by inference for
application arguments and branch joins, but is not a user-facing word.
Intersection, union, complement/top, bottom, and refinement
types are implemented. Sequences and recursive types are target (D132);
multisets and co-products are out until a consumer appears.

== 8.5 Unification

Unification is first-order, image-resident, and nominal where the term carries a
name. A term is a variable (`?name`), an atom, a compound, a typed term, or a
single-parameter binder. The substitution lives in `BindingTable`; the store is
append-only, so unification writes new entries and re-points the table. The
occurs check is enforced. `Undecided` is a named refusal, never a false.
Higher-order unification accepts Miller patterns (D59).

== 8.6 Classification

`class-of` and the entry-kind lattice classify store entries (D32). A membership
verdict is one value; an unknown kind is `UnknownKind`, not a silent extension.

== 8.7 Sequences

A sequence is a set with an order: a value plus a position function. The `Tensor`
is already ordered, so the representation is the first widening past sets. The
target adds an ordered-collection projector, order-preserving operations, and
ordered pattern support. Set operations do not apply directly: a union of
sequences is concatenation, not set union. Sequences are planned (D132).

== 8.8 Recursive types

A recursive type is the least fixed point of a type constructor: a list, a tree,
a recursive message, or the language's own expressions, patterns, classes, and
packages. The type layer needs a recursive reference and a roll/unroll or
fixpoint rule; the unifier already has an occurs check and the matcher handles
recursive compounds at run time. Recursive types are planned (D132).

= 9. Classes, slots, and instances

== 9.1 The cell model

A class is an image-resident record with a name, a superclass chain, and slots. A
slot has a name and a default. An instance is an image-resident record naming its
class and holding slot values. All of it is data in the image; the current
instance table is a root.

== 9.2 Words

`class`, `slot`, `create-instance`, `slot-value`, `set-slot-value`, `with-fields`,
`class-precedence`, `subclass-of`, `instance-of`, `metaclass`, `use-metaclass`,
and `cell-class` are the surface. `with-fields` binds a field in a body frame.

== 9.3 Dispatch

Multiple dispatch is nominal and image-resident: the generic function records its
methods, and the most specific applicable method is selected from the argument
classes' precedence order (D19, D47). The exact-specializer fast path is a cache,
not compile-time resolution; it is guarded and falls back to the dispatcher.

Method combination supports `before-method`, `after-method`, `around-method`,
`call-next-method`, and `next-method-p`. Metaclasses are the class of classes;
`cell-class` exposes the cell class closure.

== 9.4 Status

Classes, slots, instances, precedence, nominal dispatch, combination,
metaclasses, and closures are implemented (D43--D50). Linear access to instances
is enforced through the ownership layer (chapter 12); compaction and reclaim use
the same roots.

= 10. Conditions and restarts

== 10.1 Failures as values

A failure is a value with a named reason. Every word returns a `Result`; the loop
decides whether the failure is handled, becomes a supervised exit, or aborts the
run. An error is never swallowed into a false or an empty value.

== 10.2 Conditions and restarts

`condition name message` declares a condition. `restart name handler` declares a
restart. `with-handler` installs a handler for a dynamic extent. `signal` raises a
condition; `invoke-restart` transfers control to a restart. Handlers and restarts
are frame-scoped and are execution state; a handler that does not handle the
condition returns control to the search, not to a default.

== 10.3 Attribution

The condition system reports the raising layer and the rule violated. A handler
failure, a missing restart, and a condition with no handler are distinct named
refusals. The kernel's typed errors keep their family; only the ABI edge flattens
them to `(code, message)`.

= 11. Actors, channels, and supervision

== 11.1 Actors

An actor is an image-resident record with a status, a parent, a handle, a
mailbox, links, monitors, and a pending exit. The root actor is created with the
initial state. `spawn` creates a child; `self` pushes the current handle;
`yield` gives the CPU to the next ready actor; `halt` ends normally; `kill` ends
with a reason. A handle is a private capability: it is not forgeable from an
integer or a text.

== 11.2 Mailboxes and messages

`send-to` delivers to a handle; `receive-message` takes the next message (or
blocks); `receive-matching` takes the first message matching a predicate.
Reserved system messages are distinct from user messages (D75). A mailbox is a
channel; the endpoint's open flag and queued messages are image state, and only
the blocked-waiter set is host-side control state.

== 11.3 Links, exits, and monitors

`link` makes two actors' exits observable to each other; `unlink` removes it.
`trap-exit` turns an exit signal into a message; `exit-signal?` and `exit-reason`
inspect it. `monitor`/`demonitor` observe without linking; `down?`, `down-ref`,
and `down-reason` read the monitor result. `register`/`unregister`/`whereis` are
the named registry.

== 11.4 Supervision

`one-for-one`, `one-for-all`, and `rest-for-one` are the restart strategies.
`supervise` installs a strategy. A child failure is an `ErrorExit`; a deadline
exhaustion is a `TimeoutExit`; the supervisor decides whether the child restarts,
the group restarts, or the failure escalates. An unsupervised actor's failure
aborts the run.

== 11.5 Channels and ChannelEvents

`channel` creates a channel; `send`, `receive`, and `close` are the operations.
`send-event`, `receive-event`, `choose`, `wrap`, `guard`, and `with-fallback`
form ChannelEvents; `sync` is the blocking synchronization. A blocked receive is
a distinct outcome, not an error.

== 11.6 Status

Actors, mailboxes, links, trapped exits, monitors, the registry, supervision,
channels, and ChannelEvents are implemented (D20, D22, D63--D69, D75). BEAM
operational parity (copy-on-send and a per-process tracing GC) and the unmanaged
arena backend are optional. Implementation-instance IPC is a target track,
planned (D151); it extends the distribution direction recorded in D133. The
`scale_transport_send` ABI seam exists, but instance identity, discovery, remote
send/receive, delivery, partition, and security are undesigned.

== 11.7 Implementation instances and IPC

An *implementation instance* is one running actor system in a host language.
Different implementations do not share a source language, an AST, a type
hierarchy, or a dispatch model. They integrate through the actor model: an actor
handle may address an actor in another instance, and a message crosses the
instance boundary through the host adapter's transport.

A remote send carries an envelope: the target handle, the sender handle, an
optional reply channel, a portable payload, and a message identity. The envelope
and body share one message; only bulk data may be out-of-line. A payload is a
durable image entry or a canonical document; a host object, a closure, a frame,
a stack, a tensor view, or execution state never crosses. A remote handle is a
capability, not a forgeable name.

`send-to` recognizes a host-registered remote handle tensor `[RemoteHandleTag;
instance; capability]`. A tagged tensor that the host did not register is not a
capability. It records a `TransportRequest` in the scheduler and returns; the
host drains the request after the run, reads the message entry, and performs the
transport. The evaluator performs no I/O. The host delivers the numeric reply to
the sender's mailbox and resumes the sender, so `send-to; receive-message`
receives the remote result (D157, D158).

A failure is named: an unknown instance, an unreachable instance, a transport
fault, and a remote exit are distinct. A partition is an outcome, not a silent
loss. The kernel frames and checks the handle; the host adapter owns transport
semantics (`scale_transport_send`, D109). The wire encoding is the Scale actor
wire protocol, implemented in-repo and Cap'n Proto-compatible for the frozen
subset (D152, D153); compatibility is gated by a reference conformance test.
Schema tooling may take Protobuf's plugin contract and field-number metadata,
but those stay off the wire. S1 is implemented (D154): one connection, one
numeric envelope, and the root actor mailbox. S2 is implemented (D155): a
capability names a non-root worker, a monitor reports its normal exit, and an
unreachable instance is a named failure. S2.5 is implemented (D157, D158):
`send-to` accepts a remote handle and records a host transport request; the host
delivers the numeric reply back into the sender's mailbox. The host test sends
21, receives 42, receives `down normal`, and the language receives 42 on the
stack. Text replies, down delivery into the language, discovery, authentication,
remote code change, and partition semantics remain planned (D151--D153);
`docs/kernel/actor-ipc.typ` records the model and staging, and `docs/kernel/binary-format.typ`
records the wire subset and conformance plan. The local actor model
(11.1--11.5) is unchanged.

= 12. Ownership, linearity, and memory

== 12.1 Usage modes

A usage mode is a property of the type: `ExactlyOnce` for a linear resource,
`AtMostOnce` for an affine reference, and `AnyNumberOfTimes` for an immutable
image value. A linear type is a named set of traces; the end-of-trace rule is the
mode's obligation. `linearity` declares the mode and `own`, `borrow`, `consume`,
and `release` are the operations.

The checker is a quotation-trace checker. It proves a trace only when every step
is modelled; a trace containing an unmodelled word is `Undecided`, never
`Linear`. It is not wired into P4 inference and does not type arbitrary values
as linear or affine. The core therefore has a linear/affine trace discipline,
not a general linear type system.

== 12.2 Reference linearity

`reclaimable` reports whether a reference is a reclaim candidate. `compact` is
the opt-in compaction entry point; compaction preserves stable addresses and
never moves a value whose identity is observable. Ownership state is execution
state, not image data.

== 12.3 Epochs and promotion

An actor incarnation has an epoch. The scheduler records the outgoing turn's
frontier growth, resumes the incoming epoch at the current frontier, and closes
the epoch on termination. A value promoted at send or persistence is reclassified
as durable without moving; an unpromoted value in a closed epoch is reclaimable
when no live root reaches it. Reclamation reads every non-terminated actor's
suspended state; a terminated actor is not a root.

== 12.4 The arena seam

`TensorHandle` is a stable storable reference; `TensorView` is a stack-only span
projection. The managed arena allocates, resolves by generation, and invalidates
handles on close. The evaluator is not routed through the arena; this is a host
seam, not a second representation. Unmanaged storage is a later decision.

== 12.5 Frozen representation

The memory representation is frozen for the port: managed backing; shared
immutable values with promotion at every escape; no copy-on-send; no evaluator
arena routing. `Tensor` is not a `ref struct`; it cannot be stored in a class, a
map, actor state, a mailbox, a closure, or persistence. `EntryIdentity` stays a
struct. Struct and byref-like annotations are host performance choices, not
semantics.

= 13. Errors and diagnostics

== 13.1 The taxonomy

`EvaluationError` is one union with typed fault families: `UnknownKind`,
`ErrorSubject`, `ExpectedValue`, `ArityFault`, `ChannelFault`, `SortFault`,
`LimitFault`, `EnumFault`, `ActorFault`, and the other named cases. A family case
is written qualified to avoid union-case shadowing. `describeError` renders every
case; `Violations.ofEvaluationError` yields the rule string for a source
violation.

== 13.2 Diagnostics

A `Diagnostic` carries the error, the location, the baseline, and the position.
A source violation adds a character offset when source text is available
(D76). A failure at the kernel boundary is encoded as `(code, message)`; the
typed error stays inside the kernel and the Prelude.

== 13.3 Refusals

A refusal MUST name the rule and the next action, as a DU case, not a string.
`Result<_, string>` is a defect in new code. An undecidable case is reported as
`Undecided(reason)`, never as a false; an unavailable resource is `Unavailable`,
never an empty success.

The prose front-end returns `ProseError` cases (`ExpectedMatch`, `EmptyMatch`,

The taxonomy and the renderers are implemented (D94). Violation reporting covers
the source, semantic, SMT-model, and unsat-core phases (D57, D61, D62). The
prose front-end's refusals are named and tested (D142--D149). The shell
effects `run` and `capture` are Stage 1a host words (D183); the `with`
connective and the REPL are not implemented (D147).

The taxonomy and the renderers are implemented (D94). Violation reporting covers
the source, semantic, SMT-model, and unsat-core phases (D57, D61, D62).

= 14. General IR and portability

== 14.1 Four representations

A program has four representations: surface text, the elaborated term list, the
portable document, and the compilation target. The portable document is the
interchange form; the lowered instruction stream is a compilation target, not a
parallel runtime. The portable form is canonical JSON with a schema version; it
carries no host objects.

Scale has one user-facing language with three layers: Shell (interactive),
Prose (the same language without shell capabilities), and Scale (file-based
Action plus Question, with Logic definitions). They share one semantic IR; the
layers are interaction modes and documentation, not separate languages, codecs,
or lowerers.

The target runtime representation is the typed term list (D160): the program is
a `Term list`, a quotation is a `Quote of Term list`, and `apply` splices its
terms. P1 (D161) adds the core term shape and its canonical portable document;
P2 (D162) adds the kernel structural verifier, the opaque `VerifiedTermProgram`
receipt, and the `scale_verify` entry at the current ABI version; P3a (D163) adds the
token-to-term boundary; P3b (D165) makes the active program and caller frames
`Term list` and executes numbers and words. Text quotations remain until P3c,
code values are P3c, and stack typing is P4. The migration is staged in
`docs/architecture/term-ir.typ`. The differential matrix in `docs/architecture/general-ir.typ` checks the
token, expression, pattern, package, and kernel-term paths before any IR is
retired.
== 14.2 Documents

- the *pattern document*: schema version, form, kinds, predicates, clauses;
- the *expression document*: schema version, form, kinds, predicates, root;
- the *package document*: name, version, hash, imports, exports, capabilities,
  units.

Canonical constructor kinds and pinned named predicates make a document
deterministic. A pattern template is a separate open form and is refused by the
closed reader; `PatternElaboration` turns a template plus holes into a closed
pattern.

== 14.3 Capabilities and trust

Capabilities are default-deny: a package declares what it needs and a host grants
it. A host call is a named package/function request; a transport payload is a
framed target plus bytes. The host adapter contract is the six-function adapter
interface; an adapter contains no language semantics.

Capabilities belong to the retained image. A host may replace the image with
`scale_image(ImageImport)`, which validates the replacement before retaining it;
this replaces the capability surface with the new image. Scale code has no
operation that grants itself a capability.

== 14.4 Source maps

A package carries unit-scoped source maps. A violation maps back to the unit,
the location, and the character offset. A map that cannot resolve is a named
failure, not a guessed position.

== 14.5 Status

The pattern IR and portable pattern document, the expression AST and its
portable document, and the package/unit document are implemented (D96, D97). The
general expression and package nodes are implemented; the full engine contract,
the decision tree and unifier as portable components, and complete source maps
are partial. The Question surface has a symbolic parser and a first-order
evaluator for facts, rules, and questions; the question plan and ordering are not
implemented. The differential
matrix covers token, expression, pattern, package, and kernel-term paths. The
typed term list's P1 shape and portable document, P2 structural verifier,
receipt, `scale_verify` entry, P3a token-to-term boundary, P3b typed control
state, P3c quotation code values, structural history, and P3d text-channel
removal are implemented (D160--D167). P4a--P4c add the type-inference core,
expression inference, located diagnostics, and the lowering handoff (D173), but
the parser does not yet emit `parseLocated` maps and the full
`logic`/anonymous-function surface is partial. Stack typing and effect rows
remain planned.
= 15. Kernel, ABI, and the image

== 15.1 The boundary

`components/kernel/scale_abi.h` is the specification and the artifact: it declares the C ABI
and the callbacks. Anything the header declares is real; anything it does not
declare does not exist to a host adapter. The kernel exports the ABI with
`UnmanagedCallersOnly`; there is no C\# export shim. The kernel has no Prelude
state and no Prelude logic; a boundary fitness test enforces the type and
reference separation.

== 15.2 Exports

ABI version 4 declares ten exports:

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  align: (left, left),
  table.header([Export], [Contract]),
  [`scale_init`], [installs callbacks, validates the image, retains it],
  [`scale_set_callbacks`], [replaces the callback table for a live handle],
  [`scale_run`], [resolves an exported unit and executes it],
  [`scale_verify`], [verifies a term-program document and writes its receipt],
  [`scale_image`], [imports or exports the retained image; replaces one validated unit],
  [`scale_host_call`], [forwards a named package/function call],
  [`scale_transport_send`], [forwards a framed target and payload],
  [`scale_transport_acquire`], [acquires a transport resource and returns a token],
  [`scale_free`], [clears the handle and the retained image],
  [`scale_abi_version`], [returns the single ABI version],
)

The exact set and the error codes are in the header; this table is a map, not a
substitute.
== 15.3 The image

An image document is schema version 2: a form, kinds, predicates, units, groups,
exports, imports, capabilities, primitives, dependency pins, and instructions. A
unit has a name, a source hash, dependencies, explicit arity and capture slots,
and a lowered instruction list. A group names its units, their initialization
order, and each binding's classification. The loader validates names, arity,
captures, groups, kinds, predicates, jumps, capabilities, dependencies,
dependency pins, schema, and form. A call
resolves through the unit's group, pops its arity, appends its captures from the
caller's frame, and runs it; a closure invocation reuses its own frame. A short
call or a missing capture is a named refusal. The image is data; the kernel
validates it and retains it.

A dependency cycle is refused when the image is validated, not when source text
is parsed. After `scale_init`, a host may import a replacement image through
`scale_image(ImageImport)`; the kernel validates the replacement before retaining
it. `scale_image(ImageReplaceUnit)` takes one unit document, replaces the named
unit, and revalidates the whole graph before the swap. `scale_image(ImagePatchUnit)`
accepts `{"unit":...,"exports":[...],"groups":[...],"dependencyPins":{...}}`:
it can add or replace one unit, update exports and groups, and check dependency
source hashes, then revalidates the whole graph. A replacement must keep the
unit's arity and capture slots; a signature change requires whole-image import.
The image source hash is recomputed from the canonical artifact after a unit
swap. Import, replacement, and patch can return the previous image through
`out_bytes`, so the host can re-import it for rollback. The live handle retains
its execution state across image replacement; state is not serialized or
migrated between handles. The next call sees the new image. Replacement is
host-driven image swapping, not an in-language module loader.

== 15.4 The primitive wire

A primitive call crosses the ABI as tensors: a declared primitive has an arity
and a result count; the caller pops the operands, calls the host, and pushes the
results. A primitive failure carries its named reason. A result-count mismatch is
a protocol failure, never a silent truncation.

== 15.5 Invariants

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, auto),
  align: (left, left, left),
  table.header([No.], [Invariant], [Status]),
  [1], [The kernel runs without the Prelude loaded], [implemented],
  [2], [The Prelude is swappable data], [implemented],
  [3], [The kernel state has no Prelude state], [implemented],
  [4], [The kernel source contains no Prelude logic], [implemented],
  [5], [Removing a feature requires no kernel change], [partial],
  [6], [Adding a feature requires no kernel change], [partial],
  [7], [Errors are attributed to the raising layer], [partial],
  [8], [Upcalls are real], [partial],
  [9], [Version equality is enforced at load time], [partial],
)

The full table with the enforcing test or `not yet` is `docs/architecture/INVARIANTS.typ`.
D160 proposes a tenth invariant: the kernel executes only a term program with a
valid verification receipt. P2 issues and binds the receipt through
`scale_verify`; the execution refusal lands with the P3 runtime representation.
The kernel verifier is the execution authority; the Prelude may reject more but
cannot authorize a term list the kernel rejects.
= 16. The compiled subset

== 16.1 Lowering

`lowerExpression` is total over the closed `Expression` union: every constructor
either lowers or is a named `LoweringRefusal`. Supported: literals, words,
quotations, blocks, `if`, `call` over a word, `let`, `lambda`, and ground
`pattern-match`. Refused: `Pin`, `Repeated`, `Shadowing`, guards, duplicate
pattern variables, duplicate parameters, reserved binding names, calls to bound
names, and the declaration/message nodes. A refusal is a lowering-time decision;
the interpreter is the fallback.

`lowerExpression` returns a `LoweredProgram` with a unit table and entry
instructions. A lambda becomes a named unit; `MakeClosure` carries its arity.
`CallClosure` pops the arguments, refuses an underflow by name, and installs the
frame in parameter order. Enclosing lexical names are captured by free-variable
analysis (D133).

== 16.2 Slots, frames, and history

A lowered `let` writes a frame binding (for `lookup`) and a fresh slot (for a bare
name). Slots are local to a unit; a unit prologue copies its frame into slots.
The lowered instruction set carries `RecordStep` and a resumable position, so an
artifact that emits step records can replay history, bookmarks, and rollback
(D201, superseding D120). `scopeOf` reports the scope per program, and a program
without a step record stays `HistoryExcluded`. The boundaries are statements:
`lowerExpressionWithStepRecords` writes one record at each statement start of a
multi-statement `Block` or `Quotation` body, the position being the instruction
index in that sequence's own output, and a single-statement sequence records
nothing. Argument lists, message fields, and pattern clause bodies are operand
sequences rather than statements and never record, because a record there would
be a resume point with a half-built operand stack. `lowerExpression` writes no
record, so the ordinary emitter pays nothing and the interpreter's event stream
stays the only history until an emitter asks for the compiled form.

== 16.3 The IL writer

The writer is a JIT-only backend behind `EnableWriter` (Debug only by default).
The emitted method is `static KernelState<EvaluationState>
Run(KernelState<EvaluationState>)`. It threads the real store, stack, roots,
words, frames, and host, and calls the AOT-safe `WriterRuntime` for value
instructions, stack access, dispatch, and failure. Value instructions share
`LoweredAbstraction.step` with the interpreter. Slots are CLR locals. An unsupported
instruction is a named refusal. Each `LoweredUnit` becomes a method taking
`(KernelState<EvaluationState>, EntryIdentity[])`; a `MakeClosure` immediately
followed by `CallClosure` compiles to a direct call, with `EnterUnit`/`ExitUnit`
managing the frame and `PushCapture` reading it. A first-class closure is
refused (D134).

The release publish passes `EnableWriter=false`, so the writer is unreachable
from the AOT entry. A Release JIT run that needs the writer tests passes
`-p:EnableWriter=true`.

== 16.4 Status

Lowering, lambda units, pattern compilation, and the writer harness are
implemented for the supported subset. The writer emits one method per
`LoweredUnit` and compiles direct lambda calls (D134). It still refuses
first-class closures plus the tensor and pattern opcodes. The statically
referenced AOT artifact comes after the remaining opcodes.

= 17. Prose sentences and the core language

== 17.1 The core language

The core language is Scale's own concatenative surface and general IR. Basic
English and the prose surface are front ends over it; direct programmers use it
directly. It is not the unified Julia/Elixir language: that direction is dropped
(D151), and there is no shared source language, type hierarchy, or dispatch model
between implementations. Different implementations are separate instances in
their host languages and integrate through the actor model's IPC (11.7).

The Scale Prelude port is deferred until the core port is complete (D133); the
F\# reference remains the shipped Prelude.

== 17.2 Prose sentence grammar

```text
sentence    ::= statement | transition
statement   ::= quantifier subject relative? verb "."
transition  ::= "when" subject ("that")? disjunction "," "every" subject verb "."
quantifier  ::= "every" | "no" | "some"
relative    ::= "that" disjunction
disjunction ::= conjunction ("or" conjunction)*
conjunction ::= condition ("and" condition)*
condition   ::= predicate
              | "is" field
              | "has" field comparison integer
predicate   ::= a FieldName whose surface form begins with "is "
verb        ::= a FunctionName
comparison  ::= "greaterThan" | "atLeast" | "lessThan" | "atMost" | "equalTo"
integer     ::= an optionally signed base-10 integer
```

The grammar is order-sensitive: the quantifier is sentence-initial, the subject
is a type name, `that` introduces the optional relative clause, and the verb is
final. A full stop is required. There is no implicit subject and no default verb.
`and` binds tighter than `or`.

== 17.3 Quantifiers and transitions

`every` is universal affirmative; `no` is universal negative and emits a negated
postcondition under the same preconditions; `some` is existential and emits a
witness predicate with a satisfiability obligation. `all` is refused with
`UnsupportedQuantifier`; use `every`.

A transition is event-triggered: `when` is sentence-initial, the trigger and the
effect must name the same subject, and the outcome must be `every`. The trigger's
conditions are the precondition and the verb is the effect, so the emitted
property is `precondition ==> effect`.

== 17.4 Conditions

A condition is a boolean predicate phrase (`is alive`, canonicalized to
`isAlive`), an explicit copula and field (`is health`), or a numeric comparison
(`has health greaterThan 0`). A numeric comparison is a typed
`NumericCondition(field, comparison, value)`; the SMT theory is integer-valued, so
a decimal threshold is `Unavailable`, never rounded. A bare field noun is not a
condition.

== 17.5 Refused surface

Articles, pronouns, `not`/`never`, `all`, and `when` away from the sentence start
are refused with named parse errors. `or` is admitted between conditions. An
unknown word, an inactive word, a missing full stop, a misplaced token, a missing
comparison, a missing transition comma, and a mismatched transition subject each
have a named case.

== 17.6 Status

Prose sentence grammar stage 1, transitions, and disjunction are implemented (D18, D55,
D58). The grammar is intentionally a starting point, not a complete natural
language. No expansion happens until a product sentence requires it (D131); the
staged completion in `docs/language/action/prose-grammar.typ` is a map, not a commitment.

== 17.7 Prose program surface

The prose surface is a third front-end (D142--D149), documented in
`docs/language/action/prose-surface.typ`. A `.prose` file holds sentences that end with a full
stop; keywords are lowercase, and no `end` closes a block.

A match has two forms. The inline prose form uses commas to delimit the
multiple-choice construct: `match 5, when 1 then "one", otherwise "many".` The
line form is the alternative: `match 5.` followed by `when ...` and
`otherwise ...` lines. A line break does not start a new sentence, so the clause
words stay lowercase. A comma outside the choice positions is
`UnexpectedComma`; it never separates arguments.

Names are lowercase words separated by spaces, canonicalized through the
vocabulary like the Prose sentence grammar: `is alive` is the surface for the `isAlive`
token. A free multiword name is a `state of player` phrase. A `.prose` text
literal may contain spaces and keeps its case. A number is an optionally signed
base-10 literal with an optional fractional part (`3.5`, `-0.25`), parsed as a
64-bit float; a period belongs to the number only when a digit follows it, so
`1.` is the integer 1 and the full stop (contract: `docs/language/action/real-numbers.typ`). A
local binding is `let name = value in body`: the value is evaluated once, the
name is lexical and in scope in the body only, and bindings nest and shadow
(contract: `docs/language/action/local-binding.typ`). A
nested match is closed by
`close`; the line form writes `close.` and the inline form writes `close` where
the enclosing sentence continues.

A program is a sequence of sentences. A one-sentence program keeps its
expression AST; two or more become an `ExpressionAst.Block` in source order. The
expression lowers with `EvaluatorLowering` and runs on the kernel
`LoweredAbstraction` over one stack; the final stack top is the result.
`scale --prose PATH` reads and runs a `.prose` file. The parser returns
`Result<Expression, ProseError>` with named refusals. The shell effects `run`
and `capture` are host words executed by `components/shell-host` (D183): `run`
pushes the exit status, `capture` pushes standard output, and the script exits
with the last command status. Both `run("echo" "hello")` and
`run "echo" with "hello".` are accepted; the `with` connective is implemented
(D187). A REPL is not implemented (D147).

Prose has no logic definitions (D184). A `.book` file contains definitions and
compiles to a `PackageAst.PackageDocument`; a book with a `main` export is a
spell and a book without one is a library. `scale --book` classifies and, for a
spell, runs `main`; `scale --cast` calls an exported word. Prose calls a book
with `cast("path" "word" args...)`, a host word handled by
`components/book-host`. `logic` in a `.prose` file is `LogicOutsideBook`. A
book is one recursive group: self, forward, and mutual calls are ordinary calls
(D194). The optional `logic` and `cycle` markers are checked against the
definition's book calls; `and` groups cycle definitions F\#-style. A lowered word call that reaches a
runtime lexical binding is a named refusal (`DynamicCall`), not a silent value
push (D189).

A book also declares nominal constructors with `shape` (contract:
`docs/language/action/nominal-constructors.typ`). `shape point with x y.` registers the
constructor `point` with ordered fields `x` and `y`; a zero-field constructor
is `shape none.`. Declarations are book-wide, so a body may construct a shape
declared later in the file. In a book body a call to a declared shape
constructs the unification compound in the declared argument order; the arity
must equal the field count. `shape` in prose is `ShapeOutsideBook`; a duplicate
shape name is `DuplicateShape`; a shape that shares a name with a
`logic`/`cycle` definition is `ShapeNameConflict`; a mismatched construction is
`ShapeArityMismatch`. Patterns are unchanged: `point(a b)` matches positionally
in declared order. A field is read by destructuring `match` until the
image-resident registry lands (D130); a field-access word and named-pair sugar
are not provided.

A book declares generic functions and methods (contract:
`docs/language/action/multiple-dispatch.typ`). `generic area with 1.` declares a generic of
arity one; `method area(p : point) = body.` adds a method specialized on the
declared shape `point`; a method parameter binds its argument in the body.
Selection matches every argument's shape (the compound head and arity); a
missing method is `NoApplicableMethod`. The parser synthesizes one definition
per method and one dispatcher definition per generic, rewrites a generic call to
its dispatcher, and does not export the synthetic definitions. Class
specializers, cross-book methods, and method combination are not provided in
this slice.
= 18. Verification, refinement, and repair (extension)

== 18.1 SMT

Z3 is the verifier, in-process. `Microsoft.Z3` 4.12.2 is the package; `/usr/bin/z3`
4.16.0 is the offline fallback. The obligation is a typed predicate over the
field sorts. `Status.UNKNOWN` is reported, never swallowed as a pass. A resource
limit is `Unavailable`, not a proof.

== 18.2 Sorts and the lattice

The encoding carries Real, Integer, and Natural field sorts. Real is SMT Real and
QF_LRA; Integer and Natural are Int and QF_LIA; a mixed domain is QF_LIRA.
Natural is constrained to `>= 0`. The verification type lattice gives Z3,
Hedgehog, and the refiner one coordinate system (D25). A guard threshold is
validated against the field sort; the refiner uses a Real tolerance and clamps
Natural at zero.

== 18.3 Refinement and repair

The refiner's closure is proved over the domain; witness-guided guards cover
large domains; the repair search is minimum-edit and bounded. A violation reports
the phase, the source, the semantic stage, the SMT model or unsat core, the
minimum edit, and the presentation. A repair is a proposal, not a silent rewrite.

== 18.4 Decision tables

A decision table is a partition by comprehensions. The table surface, the
emitter, the Prose sentence sink, and the verified artifact are implemented
(D13, D73, D74). A table is proven where it is symbolic; it is not silently
sampled.

== 18.5 Status

SMT, the type lattice, guard bridging, strictly verified emission, violation
reporting P0--P2, the refiner, witness-guided guards, repair, and decision tables
are implemented. Arbitrary core-language programs are not proved; verification
is a feature of the specified/compiled forms, not of every program. Z3
verification is deferred while the language stabilizes (D131); the existing
features remain, but new verification scope waits.

= 19. Persistence and interchange

Persistence serializes only durable or promoted entries across the live actor and
every actor snapshot. The frontier and surviving addresses are unchanged, so the
result is a sparse image. Execution state is never serialized. A reader MUST
re-thread the association roots after loading. The image is append-only and the
portable documents are canonical JSON; a host interchange uses the package
document, not a memory dump. Persistence is implemented (D87); compaction beyond
the initial store is an extension.

A store is a versioned, length-checked binary document. Semantic corruption and
truncation are both `CorruptImageException` with a named `CorruptStore` reason.
Tensor element counts are bounded by `maxTensorElements`; interned text is bounded
by `maxTextBytes` before allocation. A reader never returns a partial store.

= 20. Versioning and conformance

== 20.1 One integer

The kernel, the Prelude image, and every host adapter share one monotonic integer
version. It is not SemVer and has no semantic meaning: it says whether two
artifacts belong to the same release. The current ABI version is 2; the image
schema moved to 2 with unit groups (D192). The image schema is 2.

The number appears in the header (`SCALE_ABI_VERSION`), in the kernel's exported
`scale_abi_version()`, and in every adapter's README. Compatibility is equality:
an adapter refuses a kernel whose version differs; the kernel refuses an image
whose schema differs. There is no partial compatibility and no matrix.
== 20.2 Release bundle

A release at version N includes the kernel, the Prelude image, and every adapter
at N. Every release bumps every artifact. The cost is a coordinated rebuild; the
benefit is that a mismatch is a deployment error, not a negotiation.

== 20.3 Conformance

The conformance test is a C harness that loads the kernel, initialises it with a
known Prelude image, wires a callback table, runs a known program that exercises a
kernel primitive and a Prelude word, verifies the result and the dispatch, checks
the version, and destroys the handle. It is the executable definition of an
adapter. It is not present yet because no adapter has landed; until then the
SageFs suite is the acceptance gate and the invariants table records the gaps.
The first target is a C adapter (D130).

The status matrix is enforced by ratchet tests: a row marked implemented or
refused has a representative check, and changing a status changes the test
(D135).

= 21. Status matrix

The suite at this state is 1741 example checks (1683 examples, 58 properties), 36 granular cases (13 snapshots, 14 integrations, 9 writer checks), and 100 discovered tests. D148 adds the lowering differential property, the prose-forms property, and the lowered-IR snapshot; D154 adds the actor IPC integration; D155 adds the capability and remote-supervision integration; D157 adds the language-level remote-send integration; D159 adds the wire golden-vector interop; D161 adds the term portable vectors; the boundary batch adds the malformed and forged-handle cases; D162 adds the P2 verifier, receipt, and `scale_verify` checks; D163 adds the P3a token boundary; D165 adds the P3b typed control-state checks; D166 adds the P3c code-value and structural-history checks; D167 adds the P3d text-refusal cleanup.

The table is generated from `docs/architecture/architecture.edn` by `tools/architecture-check.fsx`.

