= 22. Execution, effects, composition, state, and deployment

This section states the model a host can rely on. A clause is normative only
where it is marked implemented; otherwise it names the refusal or the deferred
surface.

== 22.1 Effects and purity

Effects are static labels, not runtime capabilities. `LabelSets.LabelSet<string>`
is `Closed` or `Open(known, SetVariable)`. A `Function` carries latent `effects`;
a scheme may quantify `SetVariable`; a call unions latent effects through set
unification; and a word signature carries `Effects` and `QuantifiedEffects`.
Record rows and row lacks constraints are separate from effect sets and do not
interact with them.

There is no IO monad, no purity effect, and no global purity guarantee. Effect
checking is opt-in elaboration (`scale --prose-typecheck`,
`lowerExpressionWithInference`, and book checks); the dispatcher executes words
whether or not inference modelled them. The signature registry infers the pure
core plus callable host, actor, and channel words with their capability effects;
stack, control, pattern, and declaration forms remain `NotInferred`. A word
outside the inferred registry is not a purity claim. The effectful edges are the console words, the shell `run`/`capture` host
words, `scale_host_call`/`scale_transport_send`, and the actor/channel words.
There is no first-class mutable-reference type and no general FFI primitive.
Instance slots are mutable through `set-slot-value`; that appends a new state
entry and re-threads the root.

Closed effect subtraction is implemented (`LabelSets.subtractClosed`,
`TypeRetrieval.subtractEffect`). Open subtraction is implemented through
`LabelSets.subtractOpen` and `TypeRetrieval.subtractEffectWithLacks`: it removes
the known label and adds a lacks constraint to the open remainder. A later
binding that contains the lacked label is refused. `TypeRetrieval.infer`
consumes the open rule for `with-handler` when both operands are `Code(result,
effects)` values: it subtracts `failure` from the body and adds the handler's
effects. A general handler expression and effect-label matching are not
implemented. `with-handler` and `signal` are a shallow one-shot runtime handler
over quotations. Effect rows use the open-label-set representation. Effects are
therefore tracked, not enforced; purity is a property of the inferred fragment
and the declared signatures, not of every running program.

== 22.2 Composition and modules

An image is a static set of units and groups. The loader validates names, arity,
captures, groups, kinds, predicates, jumps, capabilities, dependencies, schema,
and form; a dependency cycle is a named refusal. Units are the hot-reload
identity; a group declares initialization order. Exports, imports, and
capabilities form the package boundary; `scale_host_call` and
`scale_transport_send` are host boundaries, not language module loading.

There is no in-language dynamic loader or plugin registry. A host may replace the
retained image with `scale_image(ImageImport)`; the replacement is validated
before it is retained. `PreludeImage.replaceUnit` and
`scale_image(ImageReplaceUnit)` replace one named unit and revalidate the whole
graph before the swap. The handle retains its execution state across replacement;
state is not serialized or migrated between handles. This is host-driven image
replacement, not Scale code loading a module. Components have no
init/dispose lifecycle; the kernel handle has `scale_init`/`scale_free`, and
actors have supervision, not a component lifecycle.

== 22.3 Reversal and resources

There is no bracket/acquire-release or RAII in the core. `rollback` and bookmarks
restore execution history, and the image store is append-only; persistence is a
snapshot, not event sourcing. The ownership layer defines `ExactlyOnce`,
`AtMostOnce`, and `AnyNumberOfTimes`, with `own`, `borrow`, `consume`, `release`,
and a trace checker over quotations. That checker is a linear/affine trace
discipline; it is not wired into P4 inference and is not a general linear type
system. A trace with an unmodelled word is `Undecided`, never `Linear`. Resources
at the host boundary are released by the host or the process; the core does not
promise deterministic finalization.

== 22.4 State

Execution state is threaded through the evaluator and retained by the live
kernel handle; it is never serialized. There is no STM. Instance slots are
mutable through `set-slot-value`; the mutation appends a new state entry, so the
state is re-threaded rather than mutated in place. Actors have mailboxes,
channels, links, monitors, and supervision; actor state is execution state. Hot
reload is host-driven image replacement: the image is the hot-reload boundary,
and live programming is not specified. A new definition appends a record, and a
reader re-threads the current root. The handle keeps its execution state across
image replacement; state is not migrated between handles or serialized. Execution
does not survive a process restart unless the host reconstructs it.

== 22.5 Deployment

The intended shape is a long-running host process with a changing image or
component set, plus batch and embedded NativeAOT hosts. Each `scale_init` creates
an independent handle with its own callback table and image; multiple handles may
coexist. Multi-tenancy is not specified; one handle per tenant is the only safe
reading. Calls that share a handle are serialized by the kernel handle gate; a
host callback must not reenter the same handle. Scale code cannot change its own
capability surface: capabilities are
declared in the image and validated at load. A host can change the surface by
importing a replacement image. There is no self-modifying code, reflection-driven
capability grant, or runtime package installation.

== 22.6 Status

Effects: implemented for the inferred fragment, closed subtraction, and open
subtraction with lacks; deferred for typed handlers and effect rows. Composition:
implemented image units and groups, dependency validation, and
exports/imports/capabilities; no dynamic loader. Resources: implemented
append-only persistence, rollback, and the quotation trace checker; no
bracket/RAII and no P4 linear types. State: implemented pure threading, actor
words, handle state retention, and host-driven image replacement; no STM.
Deployment: implemented kernel
handles and NativeAOT; multi-tenancy and self-modification are out of scope.

== 22.7 Declarative IO and installation

A `.install` source is a composition root for host capabilities. It declares
`directory`, `trash`, `unlink`, and `vision` capabilities, a default delete
capability, directory policy, an undecided policy, and a confirmation policy.
The parser returns a typed `Installation`; malformed lines, unknown resource
kinds, duplicate capabilities, invalid booleans, invalid thresholds, missing
blocks, and unclosed blocks are named refusals.

An `Intent` describes a request such as removing not-pretty files. A pure
planner turns the Intent and the Installation policy into a `Plan`. The plan
requires a configured `vision` capability and evidence that names that model and
meets its threshold; it stays inside the scope directory, considers regular
files and, when `follow-links` is true, symlink entries (the link itself, never
its target; the hash is the resolved target's content hash, so `apply` still
refuses a target outside the scope), applies the recursive/hidden policy, skips
`Pretty`,
`NotApplicable`, and by default `Undecided`, and carries the confirmation
policy. A directory attribute overrides `default all`; an omitted attribute
falls back to it. A plan item carries the path, content hash, verdict, model
evidence, and action.

`apply` is the only effect boundary. It requires a confirmation receipt when the
plan requires confirmation, resolves the scope and item through real paths,
refuses a symlink escape, verifies the item hash, and then calls an
`IPlanExecutor`. The filesystem executor moves to trash by default and refuses
unlink unless explicitly enabled. A vision adapter and an Incus/mount adapter
are not implemented. Prose is not part of this contract yet.

An installation may also declare a world image, instance type, CPU/memory/disk
limits, and network. `renderIncusProfile` emits a deterministic Incus profile
from those fields; it does not call Incus. The mount/watch adapter remains
future work.

